      "parent": 468,
      "message": {
        "role": "tool",
        "tool_call_id": "bdcbe0f9-0565-471c-8495-d218173a0739",
        "content": "=== STAIRS ===\n# stairs\n\nStairs in NetHack come in two glyphs: > for stairs down and < for stairs up. To descend to the next dungeon level, stand ON a > tile and call descend. Critical: < (stairs up) do NOT take you deeper. The goal is to descend through > tiles.\n\nIf descend fails ('you cannot go down here'), you are not on a > tile. Check UNDER PLAYER in the observation — it says 'stairs DOWN' or 'stairs UP' or the tile you are actually on. The @ glyph hides the tile beneath it.\n\nTo find stairs: scan VISIBLE FEATURES (lists every stairs/altar/fountain with (x,y) coords). Call move_to(x, y) to walk to a > tile, then descend.\n\n\n\n=== HIDDEN PASSAGE ===\n# hidden passage\n\nA hidden passage is a corridor or door that is not visible until searched. Many NetHack levels have hidden passages, especially the Gnomish Mines and dead-end corridors. To find them, stand adjacent to a suspected wall and call search repeatedly. Each search has roughly a 1/6 chance to reveal each hidden adjacent square, so 5-10 searches in one spot usually works.\n\nUse search(times=10) or search(times=20) to batch keystrokes. A hidden door shows as | or - in the wall; a hidden corridor shows as # in solid rock. Trapdoors and pit traps are also revealed by searching.\n\nTactics: when autoexplore reports no frontier and no > is visible, assume a hidden corridor or trapdoor. Walk to a dead-end and search 10-20 times. Mine Town and the Oracle level frequently have hidden passages.\n\n",
        "name": "ipython"
      },
      "sampled": false,
      "timestamp": 1785608825.9706118,
